  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date, federal contract obligations total $482B. The largest single-state recipients are Texas at $70.9B and Virginia at $67.8B, followed by California at $40.7B, Maryland at $23.7B, and Arizona at $22.6B. Other states in the top ten are Florida at $21.2B, District Of Columbia at $20.5B, Pennsylvania at $18.4B, Connecticut at $15.4B, and New Mexico at $13.2B. These figures are contract obligations and represent partial fiscal year totals, since current-year totals are fiscal year to date. Contractors should treat these numbers as activity to date rather than full-year outcomes, and use them to identify heavy federal spending concentrations across states for business development and capture planning.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which states lead federal contract obligations?",
        "body": "Texas and Virginia lead the list, with Texas at $70.9B and Virginia at $67.8B. California is next at $40.7B, then Maryland at $23.7B and Arizona at $22.6B. The top ten also include Florida, District Of Columbia, Pennsylvania, Connecticut, and New Mexico."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What should contractors take from FY2026 to date totals?",
        "body": "These totals reflect fiscal year to date contract obligations, not final annual totals. Use the listed state figures to focus business development where obligations are already largest, while remembering that midyear numbers can change before year end."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Which state gets the most federal contract money?",
      "a": "Texas receives the most federal contract money. Texas leads with $70.9B in contract obligations for FY2026 to date."
